    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d538">

                <pb facs="1292" n="1178" sameAs="frus:frus1943/pg1178"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Roosevelt Papers: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">President Roosevelt to Prime Minister Churchill
                        </hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d538-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hyde
                                Park</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian" when="1943-08-26"
                            >26 August 1943</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>From the President to Colonel Warden, Personal and Secret.</p>
                <p>Number 346. Your number 414.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d538-f2"/> Wednesday, the
                    First, is all right in every way. If the Subaltern wants to go to
                        Oglethorpe<anchor n="3" xml:id="d538-f3"/> it would give her more time in
                    Washington if she were to come down a day or two ahead.</p>
                <p>I hope Lady Warden<anchor n="4" xml:id="d538-f4"/> is getting a real rest and
                    that you are too. Also I hope that you have gone to one of the lakes. Be sure to
                    have the big one weighed and verified by Mackenzie.<anchor n="5"
                        xml:id="d538-f5"/>
                </p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d538-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d538-f1-content">
                <p>Sent to the White House Map Room in Washington as telegram No. <hi
                        rendition="#small-caps">Black</hi> 14. Delivered to the British Embassy at
                    Washington for transmission to Churchill in Canada.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d538-f2" n="2" xml:id="d538-f2-content">
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Supra.</hi>
                </p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d538-f3" n="3" xml:id="d538-f3-content">
                <p>Churchii’s daughter Mary visited the Women’s Army Corps at Oglethorpe, Georgia,
                    August 31–September 2, 1943.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d538-f4" n="4" xml:id="d538-f4-content">
                <p>Mrs. Churchill.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d538-f5" n="5" xml:id="d538-f5-content">
                <p>Prime Minister Mackenzie King.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
